+# Agent Note: Liquid Glass web skin ownership
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-17-liquid-glass-web-skin.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The web profile's experimental wallpaper mounted a canvas in the HTML shell and selected the first canvas on the page. That placed visual state outside Cordis lifecycle ownership, allowed duplicate canvases after reload, and painted an opaque overlay over Harness surfaces.
+
+## Decision
+
+The `dsh-liquid-wallpaper-skin` client plugin owns the `#dsh-liquid-background` canvas, its stylesheet, and the fluid engine's start and cleanup effects. The web frontend serves the existing Pavel Dobryakov simulation as an asset; the plugin loads it only after creating its owned canvas. Theme and component packages own the Liquid Glass tokens and surface material, while conversation content remains DOM text above the fluid layer.
+
+The fluid engine caps device-pixel ratio at 1.5, lowers simulation work for reduced motion, injects slow cyan-to-blue ambient splats, pauses while the document is hidden, and releases the WebGL context during teardown. WebGL and backdrop-filter failures fall back to a static Deep Ocean background and higher-opacity surfaces.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**HTML-owned canvas:** Rejected because it has no plugin unload boundary, races the app's own canvases, and makes HMR duplicate global listeners.
+
+**A WebGL context per glass component:** Rejected because DOM capture and context multiplication would increase GPU cost and couple presentation components to a renderer.
+
+**Opaque recoloring of every component:** Rejected because it hides the fluid world and turns the conversation into a grid of cards; the tokens keep the transcript transparent and reserve stronger material for floating surfaces.
+
+## Consequences
+
+An SVG displacement enhancement was tested on composer and modal surfaces, but browser compositing placed the composer above menus and modal controls. It is therefore disabled in favor of CSS edge reflections, while the preserved `lg_fragment_main.glsl` remains the source for a future shared texture renderer rather than forcing DOM capture into the current slot architecture. The web asset path is part of the frontend build and must remain available to the profile server.
