+# Agent Note: Host-backed Liquid Glass runtime controls
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-18-liquid-glass-runtime-controls.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The Deep Ocean skin has tunable Fluid constants but no product-owned control path. Editing the public script or an out-of-tree skin bundle makes brightness changes immediate only after a reload, provides no validation, and cannot preserve one preference across the Sidebar and Settings views. Recreating the renderer for every control change would also risk duplicate Canvas nodes, listeners, and WebGL contexts.
+
+## Decision
+
+`@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-liquid-glass` owns the `ui-liquid-glass` Settings namespace, one browser controller, and both UI entries. `sidebar.footer.action` carries the compact high-frequency controls; `settings.general.item` carries all presentation, palette, and quality controls. Both entries receive the same observable state and callbacks through the slot inject hooks compartment.
+
+The Host API Proxy includes `ui-liquid-glass` in its explicit Web settings allowlist, so loopback browsers can describe and mutate the registered section without exposing unrelated Settings namespaces.
+
+The controller owns `#dsh-liquid-background`, `/fluid-wallpaper-script.js`, delayed Host writes, and the public Fluid runtime object. Slider input updates the shared snapshot synchronously, coalesces Canvas and engine changes to one animation frame, and persists after 180 ms without sending one Settings mutation per pointer event. Reset clears user fields instead of writing default values, so a deployment can replace its composition defaults later.
+
+The Fluid runtime exposes bounded normalized motion, pointer, Bloom, palette, quality, and enable controls. Quality changes reuse its single WebGL context and rebuild framebuffers. Disable and hidden-tab states stop the animation-frame chain; disposal releases listeners, framebuffers through context loss, the script, and the Canvas. CSS owns light-mode attenuation and the static Deep Ocean fallback.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Keep manual source edits.** This leaves no discoverable UI, validation, persistence, or shared state and requires a reload for every comparison.
+
+**Store values only in localStorage.** This would bypass the Harness Settings owner, diverge across browsers and ports, and make reset semantics independent from deployment defaults.
+
+**Let the out-of-tree skin own the controls.** A handwritten external bundle cannot participate in the typed slot and Settings APIs without duplicating the in-tree client architecture. The compatibility skin defers when the in-tree owner is active.
+
+**Create a WebGL context per refracting component.** The controls tune one shared Fluid renderer. Composer and overlay refraction remain CSS Glass until a shared texture-based refraction pass can preserve DOM hit testing.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Fluid brightness and motion become live, validated product preferences with one source of truth and two purpose-specific views. The browser still owns one Canvas and one WebGL context, while quality changes have an explicit framebuffer rebuild cost. The new package adds a Host settings namespace and a browser plugin to the shipped Web roster. Palette transitions remain gradual because existing dye dissipates instead of being cleared.
